course: dictionary
lesson: 7696
title: Proverbs: wise sayings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A proverb is a short, well-known saying that gives advice or states a truth about life. «Look before you leap» does not mean you should look down before you jump. It means think before you act.
explain: Proverbs rarely mean exactly what their words say. To understand one, ask yourself what the saying is really telling you. Grown-ups use them to give advice, so the meaning is often a lesson about how to behave.
explain: Proverbs are also the name of a book in the Bible, full of wise sayings, many linked with King Solomon. Readings from it teach about wisdom, honesty and hard work. One example is «Speak up for those who cannot speak for themselves» (Proverbs 31:8).
fact: A proverb is a short, well-known saying that gives advice or states a truth about life.
fact: A proverb's meaning is often different from its literal words.
fact: «Look before you leap» is a proverb that means think before you act.
fact: Proverbs (with a capital P) is a book of the Old Testament full of wise sayings, many linked with King Solomon.
fact: «Whoever conceals their sins does not prosper» (Proverbs 28:13) teaches that hiding wrongdoing does not lead to a good outcome.
exercise: «Start children off on the way they should go» (Proverbs 22:6) is an example of what? | A long story about a king | *A proverb, a short saying that gives advice | A type of shop | A sum of money
exercise: «Look before you leap» means what? | Jump as high as you can | *Think before you act | Never look at anything | Look down before you jump
exercise: Which book of the Bible is full of wise sayings linked with King Solomon? | Psalms of David | *Proverbs | Genesis | Exodus
test: Why do grown-ups use proverbs? | To make the room darker | To give advice | To sing songs | *To give advice about how to behave
test: What does «Speak up for those who cannot speak for themselves» (Proverbs 31:8) teach? | *Stand up for people who cannot stand up for themselves | Never speak in class | Only grown-ups may talk | Speak as loudly as you can
exam: Why should you not take a proverb word for word? | *A proverb's meaning is often different from its literal words | A proverb's meaning is hidden in the saying, so you must work out what it teaches | Proverbs are always about animals | Proverbs are only used in maths
